---
import Scene from '../Scene.astro';
import Tex from '../Tex.astro';
import Slider from '../Slider.astro';
import Readout from '../Readout.astro';
import Segmented from '../Segmented.astro';
---
<Scene id="multiple" figure="square" label="In 3D, the surface z equals x plus the square root of y over the rectangle from x 0 to 2 and y 1 to 4, filled with an n by n grid of blue columns whose total volume approaches 46 thirds. A slice sweeps across to show the iterated integral. In polar mode, an ice cream cone shape between a cone and a sphere is filled with wedge-shaped columns whose total approaches 4 pi times root 2 minus 1, over 3, over a disk, one wedge highlighted with sides delta r and r delta theta.">
  <div class="beat" data-beat="Volume under a surface">
    <p>One integral measures area under a curve. Two measure volume under a <span class="k-c1">surface</span>. Take <Tex t="f(x, y) = \cA{x + \sqrt y}"/> over the rectangle <Tex t="R = [0,2]\times[1,4]"/> on the floor. How much space sits between them?</p>
    <p class="aside">Drag the figure to turn it.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="beat" data-beat="Columns">
    <p>Do what worked in one variable: chop and add. Cut <Tex t="R"/> into an <Tex t="n\times n"/> grid of small rectangles, each with area <Tex t="\Delta A = \Delta x\,\Delta y"/>. Over each one stand a <span class="k-c3">column</span> whose height is <Tex t="f"/> at the cell’s corner nearest the origin.</p>
    <Tex display t="V \approx \sum_{i,j} \cA{f(x_i, y_j)}\,\cC{\Delta A}"/>
    <p>Since <Tex t="f"/> grows in both <Tex t="x"/> and <Tex t="y"/>, every column fits under the surface, so this sum undershoots. With four columns it is about <Tex t="10.74"/>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="beat" data-beat="More columns">
    <p>Refine the grid. The columns get thinner, the gaps under the surface shrink, and the sum climbs toward a single number. That limit is the double integral:</p>
    <Tex display t="\iint_R f\,dA = \lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{i,j} f(x_i,y_j)\,\Delta A = \frac{46}{3}"/>
    <p class="aside">Drag the slider back and forth and watch the readout. At <Tex t="n = 30"/> there are 900 columns.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="beat" data-beat="Slice by slice">
    <p>To actually compute it, slice. Freeze <Tex t="y"/> and the cross-section under the surface is a plain region with area</p>
    <Tex display t="\cB{A(y)} = \int_0^2 (x + \sqrt y)\,dx = 2 + 2\sqrt y"/>
    <p>Sweep the <span class="k-c2">slice</span> from <Tex t="y = 1"/> to <Tex t="y = 4"/> and add up the slices, just like a volume by cross-sections:</p>
    <Tex display t="\int_1^4 \cB{(2 + 2\sqrt y)}\,dy = 6 + \tfrac43\cdot 7 = \frac{46}{3}"/>
    <p class="aside">The inner integral treats <Tex t="y"/> as a constant and gives an expression in <Tex t="y"/>, and the outer integral finishes the job. Slicing the other way gives the same answer.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="beat" data-beat="Round regions">
    <p>A round region is awkward to cut into rectangles, so cut it with circles and rays. Take the classic ice cream cone: the solid above the <span class="k-c5">cone</span> <Tex t="z = r"/> and below the <span class="k-c1">sphere</span> <Tex t="x^2+y^2+z^2 = 2"/>, over the disk <Tex t="r \le 1"/>.</p>
    <p>Each wedge column runs from the cone up to the sphere, a height of <Tex t="\sqrt{2-r^2} - r"/>. Adding up the wedges gives</p>
    <Tex display t="\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^1 \left(\sqrt{2-r^2}-r\right) r\,dr\,d\theta = \frac{4\pi(\sqrt2-1)}{3} \approx 1.735"/>
    <p class="aside">The inner integral is <Tex t="\left[-\tfrac13(2-r^2)^{3/2} - \tfrac13 r^3\right]_0^1 = \tfrac{2\sqrt2 - 2}{3}"/>, and multiplying by <Tex t="2\pi"/> gives the volume. The wedge sums settle on <Tex t="1.735"/>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="beat" data-beat="Where the r comes from">
    <p>Look at one <span class="k-c2">wedge</span>. Its floor is almost a rectangle, with one side <Tex t="\Delta r"/> and the other an arc of length <Tex t="r\,\Delta\theta"/>. So its area is about <Tex t="r\,\Delta r\,\Delta\theta"/>, and wedges far from the center are bigger. That is the <Tex t="r"/> in</p>
    <Tex display t="dA = r\,dr\,d\theta"/>
    <p>Leave it out and every wedge is weighted as if its floor had area <Tex t="\Delta r\,\Delta\theta"/>, however far out it sits, so the wedges near the center count far too much. The sum then heads to <Tex t="\pi^2/2 \approx 4.93"/> instead of <Tex t="1.735"/> (the rose readout).</p>
    <p class="aside">In general, a change of variables stretches area by the Jacobian, <Tex t="dA = \left|\partial(x,y)/\partial(u,v)\right| du\,dv"/>. For polar coordinates it is <Tex t="r"/>. For example, <Tex t="x = 2u + v,\ y = u + 2v"/> has Jacobian <Tex t="3"/>, and <Tex t="\iint_R (x+y)\,dA = 3"/>.</p>
  </div>
  <Tex slot="takeaway" display t="\iint_R f\,dA = \int_c^d\!\!\int_a^b f(x,y)\,dx\,dy, \qquad dA = r\,dr\,d\theta"/>
  <Fragment slot="controls">
    <Segmented name="region" label="Region" options={[['rect', 'Rectangle'], ['polar', 'Polar']]}/>
    <Slider name="n" label="n" min={1} max={30} step={1} value={4} digits={0} color="c3"/>
    <Readout name="sum" label="Sum of columns" color="c3"/>
    <Readout name="exact" label="Exact" color="c1"/>
    <Readout name="nor" label="Without the r" color="c4"/>
  </Fragment>
</Scene>
